
==================================================================
2026-11-17 - SEO fixes from the client's checklist
==================================================================

WHAT WAS ASKED
The client sent 7 points. I first checked which were real, then fixed these:

1) Breadcrumb 404  (6 files)
   Files: blogs/<slug>.html and <slug>/index.html for the 3 blog articles.
   Breadcrumb = the small "Home > Blog > Article" trail. It is also written as
   JSON-LD (structured data that Google reads). One item pointed to /insights/,
   a page that does not exist. Changed it to https://www.greetsequipment.com/blog
   (/blog, not /blog.html, because .htaccess redirects blog.html -> /blog).

2) Office address  (224 tracked files, incl. build_pages.js)
   Old: "Shop No 23, 1st Floor, above Punjab National Bank, near BDA Complex, ..."
   New: "No.56, 1st Floor, 12th Main Road, Sector 6, HSR Layout, Bangalore - 560102 (Above Punjab National Bank)"
   The address lives in 3 shapes:
     a) plain text in the footer / contact page  -> replaced with the new line
     b) text split over two lines (blog footers)  -> regex with \s+ matches any whitespace
     c) JSON-LD "streetAddress" (street only; city and postcode are separate fields)
        -> street = "No.56, ..., HSR Layout (Above Punjab National Bank)", and
           addressLocality changed Bengaluru -> Bangalore to match.
   build_pages.js was changed too, otherwise regenerating pages would bring the old
   address back. I left admin-server/backups/ alone (old CMS snapshots).

3) Duplicate product pages  (2 files + sitemap)
   Two URLs for one product each said "I am the original" (self canonical).
   Chosen main pages: /novatec/2crd/ and /huasheng/ltmc/.
   The long-URL twins now have <link rel="canonical"> and og:url pointing at the
   main page, which tells Google "index the other one, not me". Also removed the
   two long URLs from sitemap.xml (a sitemap should list only canonical pages).

4) non-www -> www redirect  (.htaccess)
   RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^greetsequipment\.com$ [NC]   <- only when host has no www
   RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://www.greetsequipment.com/$1 [R=301,L]  <- send to www, keep path
   R=301 = permanent redirect; L = stop processing rules. It sits first so it
   runs before the other rules. Needs testing on the live server.

5) sitemap.xml: /blog.html -> /blog (same reason as the breadcrumb).

NOT CHANGED (already fine / not code)
  - Blog canonical + JSON-LD url: already correct (/<slug>/).
  - Blog URLs in sitemap: already present.
  - Point 7 (Web3Forms / hosting / domain ownership): an admin task, not code.

HOW TO VERIFY
  git grep -E "Shop No 23|BDA Complex"        -> nothing (except backups)
  curl -I http://greetsequipment.com/         -> 301 to https://www.greetsequipment.com/

------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-11-17 (later) - Correction: duplicate product pages
------------------------------------------------------------------
I had pointed the long product pages' canonical at /novatec/2crd/ and /huasheng/ltmc/.
That was wrong. Reading build_pages.js showed how the site is built:
  - /novatec/2crd/  and /huasheng/ltmc/  = CATEGORY pages (a listing with one product card)
  - /novatec/2crd-series-.../ and /huasheng/ltmc-low-.../ = MACHINE pages (full specs)
A canonical says "this other URL is the real one". Pointing the full product page at a thin
listing page would make Google index the listing and drop the product page.
Fix (the "differentiate" option):
  - Reverted canonical + og:url on the two machine pages (each canonical to itself again).
  - Restored their 2 entries in sitemap.xml (now 82 URLs).
  - Gave the category pages their own titles, so the pair no longer looks the same:
      2CRD: "2CRD Series Range – Novatec Vacuum Cleaning Machines | Greets Equipment India"
      LTMC: "LTMC Coating Equipment Range – Huasheng | Greets Equipment India"
    plus a new description for the 2CRD category (the machine page already had its own).
  - Same wording put in products.js ("seo_title"/"seo_desc" on the category) because
    build_pages.js reads titles from there; otherwise a re-run would undo the HTML edit.
WARNING: do not re-run build_pages.js yet - it rewrites sitemap.xml without the blog pages.

CORRECTION: build_pages.js already lists the blog pages in the sitemap, so re-running it is safe. Only /blog.html -> /blog was changed there.

------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-11-17 - Mobile check + touch-target pass (styles.css, end of file)
------------------------------------------------------------------
Method: opened all pages in headless Chrome at phone widths and measured overflow, tap
targets and table behaviour. Found small tap targets (menu button 28x20px, breadcrumb and
footer links ~19px tall) -> added one @media (max-width:768px) block at the end of
styles.css: 48px menu hit area (needs !important because some pages carry an inline copy
of that CSS), 40-44px min-height for crumbs/back/footer links, lighter hero accent word.

BUG on machine pages (e.g. /novatec/pluritank-ultrasonic-cleaning-line/): content cut off on
the right on phones. Cause: mobile grid used "grid-template-columns: 1fr" (= minmax(auto,1fr)),
so the column grew to its widest content (the process table, ~488px). Sideways overflow is
hidden on this site, so it was clipped, not scrollable. Fix: minmax(0,1fr) on .mlayout plus
min-width:0 on .mleft/.mright/.mcols/.mcol-card.

TABLES: "spec" tables fit. Wide "model" tables (up to 16 columns) scroll inside
.m-models-table-wrap; added a "Swipe sideways" hint (main.js, only when overflowing), a faded
right edge and a sticky first column.
"Version / Notes" column: the green pill (.pill-orientation) was squeezed in the narrow column,
so text like "Rotation and tilting" spilled out of it. On phones the pill now wraps its text,
has softer corners, and the notes column gets min-width 104px (td:nth-last-child(2)).

FOOTER (224 pages + build_pages.js): bold "Contact" heading above the phone numbers and a plain
"Email:" label before the email address.

/novatec/2crd/ title set back to "2CRD single-chamber vacuum cleaning - Novatec | Greets
Equipment India" (HTML + products.js); its description keeps the new category wording.

2026-11-17: Footer CIN: and GSTIN: labels made bold (<strong>) on all pages + build_pages.js, matching Registered/Corporate Office labels.

------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-11-19 - Redirect all contact.html?page=... URLs to /contact (.htaccess)
------------------------------------------------------------------
Problem: product pages link to contact.html?page=<product>&category=...&brand=..., so Google
sees hundreds of duplicate "contact" URLs.

Fix (added to .htaccess, ABOVE the existing contact.html rule - Apache runs rules top to bottom):
  RewriteCond %{QUERY_STRING} .
      -> "only continue if the URL has a query string (at least one character after ?)".
  RewriteRule ^contact(\.html)?/?$ /contact? [R=301,L]
      -> matches contact, contact.html, contact/ ; sends a 301 (permanent) to /contact.
         The "?" at the end of the target tells Apache to DROP the old query string
         (normally Apache keeps it). R=301 = permanent redirect, L = last rule.
No loop: after the redirect /contact has no query string, so the condition fails.

TRADE-OFF: main.js (~line 308) reads ?page=/category=/brand= to pre-fill the enquiry form and
tag which product a lead came from. After this redirect that info is lost for visitors who
click "Enquire" on a product page. Alternative that keeps it: leave links alone and add
<link rel="canonical" href="https://www.greetsequipment.com/contact"> to contact.html.
Test after deploy: curl -I "https://www.greetsequipment.com/contact.html?page=x&brand=y"
should show 301 and Location: /contact.

------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-11-19 - Redirect index.html?ind=... URLs to the home page (.htaccess)
------------------------------------------------------------------
Same idea as the contact rule. Added above the existing index.html rule:
  RewriteCond %{QUERY_STRING} .      -> only when the URL has a query string
  RewriteRule ^index\.html$ /? [R=301,L]  -> permanent redirect to "/", trailing ? drops the query.
(The old rule below it only redirects index.html with NO query string; it can't match these
because Apache keeps the query on its target - that is why a separate rule was needed.)
TRADE-OFF: products.js (checkUrlInd, ~line 6406) reads ?ind= to pre-select the industry filter
when someone clicks an industry link in the footer from another page. After the redirect they
land on the plain home page with no filter chosen (the #finder fragment still scrolls them to
the finder). Alternative: canonical tag on index.html pointing to https://www.greetsequipment.com/.
Test: curl -I "https://www.greetsequipment.com/index.html?ind=dies" -> 301, Location: /

------------------------------------------------------------------
2026-11-19 - Fix Search Console "price required for Offer" (Product snippet / Merchant listing)
------------------------------------------------------------------
Cause: every machine page's Product JSON-LD had an "offers": {"@type":"Offer","priceCurrency":"INR",
"availability":...,"seller":...} block but NO price. Google says an Offer must have a price, so
it flagged each page twice (Product snippet + Merchant listing).
The site is quotation-based (no prices), and Google's advice is to remove incomplete Offer markup
rather than invent a price. So:
 1. build_pages.js (the generator, productSchema ~line 754): removed the "offers" property.
 2. All 194 generated machine pages: removed the identical ,"offers":{...} text from the
    JSON-LD by a one-off find-and-replace script (JSON stays valid; Product keeps name,
    description, image, brand).
After deploy: in Search Console open each issue and click "Validate fix"; it takes days/weeks.
Products without offers are still valid for normal rich results, just not Merchant listings
(which need a price anyway).
